  "footnote": "Source: OpenBook (company figures), via the German Book Information Centre",
  "notes": "Where books sell, and what sells. In 2025 content commerce, the apps that sell through recommended videos and posts, such as Douyin, Kuaishou and Xiaohongshu, sold 40.53% of books by list price and became the largest channel for the first time. Four years earlier it was 16.42%. Traditional online marketplaces fell from 45.06% to 32.37%. Bookstores held 13.65% in 2025. Their share rose from 11.93% to 13.99% between 2023 and 2024, but that crosses OpenBook's restatement, and bookstore sales themselves kept falling. As for what sells: children's books were 28.79% of list-price sales and study aids 26.47%, together more than half, while literature was 8.84%. In 2024 the average discount in content commerce was over 60%, so books there sold below 40% of list price, the same story as the deepening discounts on the previous page. Source details: OpenBook, as reported in the German Book Information Centre Beijing's Buchmarkt China 2025."
